Ask Music Assistant for the visualiser and colour roles.

The daemon hardcodes [Roles.PLAYER], so the ring could never be driven from
the music: Music Assistant supports visualizer@v1 and color@v1 and grants both
the moment they are requested, but nothing was asking. Verified against a live
server, which replied

  active_roles=['player@v1','controller@v1','color@v1','visualizer@v1','metadata@v1']

and then sent VISUALIZATION_LOUDNESS, VISUALIZATION_SPECTRUM (12 bins, the
count requested here) and VISUALIZATION_BEAT.

The roles have to go on the PLAYER rather than a client of our own. A
standalone VISUALIZER+COLOR client is legal and the server grants it both
roles, but it never receives a stream/start, so _current_visualizer_config
stays None and every frame is dropped before the callback. Measured: the
standalone client received colour and zero frames; the player received all
three feeds.

n_disp_bins is 12 because the ring has twelve segments, so the server does the
band mapping and the device does not have to. Everything else here follows
sendspin's own reference client in sendspin/tui/app.py: all six types, a
65536 buffer, and a mel scale from 20 Hz to 20 kHz. METADATA and CONTROLLER are requested unconditionally, not only when
MPRIS works: there is no session bus here, so the upstream condition meant
the server was never asked for its metadata or controller state at all.

We had asked for four
types, which is why f_peak and pitch never arrived - the server was not
withholding them.

Everything else lives in biscuit_sendspin_viz, which this only calls, so that
the vendored application carries as little of ours as possible.

